WEBVTT

00:00:00.206 --> 00:00:04.056
Training every weight from random values
needs a lot of data.

00:00:04.056 --> 00:00:08.406
But a network trained on a large dataset
has already done most of the work.

00:00:08.406 --> 00:00:13.294
Its early layers have learned general
features, like edges and textures.

00:00:13.294 --> 00:00:16.381
Only the final head is tied to the old
classes.

00:00:16.386 --> 00:00:21.386
So keep the backbone, take off the old
head, and attach a new one for your
classes.

00:00:21.386 --> 00:00:22.761
Here, ten.

00:00:22.761 --> 00:00:25.142
The backbone hands over 512 features,

00:00:25.143 --> 00:00:28.361
and the new head turns them into ten class
scores.

00:00:28.374 --> 00:00:31.833
To use the backbone as a feature
extractor, freeze it,

00:00:31.834 --> 00:00:34.012
so only the head's weights change.

00:00:34.011 --> 00:00:37.911
Each of the ten outputs has 512 weights
and one bias:

00:00:37.911 --> 00:00:40.524
5130 parameters to train.

00:00:40.514 --> 00:00:45.527
Now swap in a backbone ten times larger
that still gives 512 features.

00:00:45.527 --> 00:00:48.502
How many parameters do you train while it
is frozen?

00:00:48.502 --> 00:00:50.189
Pause and decide.

00:00:51.673 --> 00:00:55.123
Still 5130, however large the backbone is.

00:00:55.123 --> 00:00:56.811
Fine-tuning goes further:

00:00:56.811 --> 00:00:59.423
it unfreezes the backbone and keeps
training.

00:00:59.423 --> 00:01:02.436
But use a learning rate about ten times
smaller.

00:01:02.436 --> 00:01:04.136
The weights are already good.

00:01:04.136 --> 00:01:06.586
A large step throws them out of the
valley.

00:01:06.586 --> 00:01:08.398
A small step adapts them.

00:01:08.398 --> 00:01:10.347
And wait until the head has settled,

00:01:10.348 --> 00:01:13.974
because a random head sends large
gradients back into good weights.

00:01:13.959 --> 00:01:16.409
Two things stay your responsibility.

00:01:16.409 --> 00:01:19.759
The preprocessing the weights were trained
with is part of the model:

00:01:19.759 --> 00:01:23.384
input size, colour order and
normalization.

00:01:23.384 --> 00:01:26.672
And pretrained weights do not replace
evaluation.

00:01:26.672 --> 00:01:28.570
Keep an independent test set,

00:01:28.571 --> 00:01:32.235
and compare frozen against fine-tuned on
the same split.
